{
 "study": "Is there a rule faster than an hour worth paper on Revolut X? (2026-09-21)",
 "method": {
  "data": "one year of Coinbase 15-minute candles per symbol (34,993 bars, 2025-09-21 to 2026-09-20), the Kraken proxy; the 60-minute rows are the same bars aggregated",
  "fills": "the loop's own: every order takes the touch \u2014 half the measured Revolut X spread plus the 9 bps taker fee, each way; entries at the next bar's open",
  "stops": "8 % floor under cost and a 3xATR(14) trail from the high since entry, read against each bar's low; two-bar re-entry cooldown after any exit",
  "rules": {
   "trend": "fast SMA over slow SMA and a close above the prior N-bar high to enter; exit on the cross back or a close under the 20-bar low. Grid: fast 10/20/30, slow 50/100/200, breakout 20/55",
   "pullback": "RSI(2) below an entry level with the close above a slow SMA to enter; exit when RSI(2) crosses an exit level, after a maximum hold, or on a close under the SMA. Grid: SMA 100/200, entry 5/10/20, exit 60/70/80, max hold 8/16 bars"
  },
  "walkForward": "parameters chosen on the first eight months by return over drawdown; the last four months reported out of sample; buyHoldOOS is the same window held from its first open, one round trip of costs",
  "script": "scratch, not shipped: m15/freq_study.py (the same harness as the reference's \u00a73.5 studies)"
 },
 "verdict": "Nothing survives. At 15 minutes the trend rule's OWN BEST in-sample parameters lose on every coin, in sample and out; the pullback rule loses 60-90 % because 600 round trips at ~20 bps each is the account. At 60 minutes every in-sample fit loses too, so the positive out-of-sample rows (18-27 trades) are selection noise, not an edge \u2014 and buy-and-hold beat two of the three. The constraint is the round-trip cost, not the model's speed.",
